{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Two Phase-4 REJECTIONS, recorded so a future janitor run does not re-raise them. (1) The gerrit-verify.yaml concurrency guard (lines 40-43): group keyed on GERRIT_CHANGE_ID with cancel-in-progress:true, so any re-dispatch for a change cancels its queued predecessor and a cancelled run posts Verified -1. Observed live during a ~2h GitHub hosted-runner outage on change 1452, which then merged on a human-cast Verified +1 with no CI run. REJECTED by the maintainer - do not re-file. (2) A separate validation gate on remediation proposals - i.e. scoring 'is the observation true' independently from 'is the recommendation right'. The OSS-practice survey found no system does this and called the split 'genuinely novel' for a recommendations pipeline, but the janitor's own coverage analysis showed all four observed error classes are already handled upstream by the subject-scoped intent check, the harm-reachability sub-question, and the community-vs-project proposer decorrelation. Building a pass for an unobserved failure mode is the speculative structure the skill's own concern #8 warns against. REJECTED - do not re-raise without an observed instance."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786057898695072001,"uuid":"f45ac2d2-403e-41b8-83a1-85375615892b"}